mode: "100644"
text: "# Calendar Structure\n\nOnce cadence is set, design the **recurring skeleton** — the repeatable structure that means you're\nnever staring at a blank page. This is what a calendar *is*: not a list of posts, but a system that\ngenerates them.\n\n## Buckets, not specific posts\n\nA content calendar defines **recurring slots** (buckets), each tied to a pillar and a format — not\nindividual captions. Example for a 4×/week LinkedIn + 3×/week TikTok founder:\n\n- **Mon (LinkedIn):** teaching post from Pillar A (how-to)\n- **Tue (TikTok):** storytime from Pillar B\n- **Wed (LinkedIn):** contrarian POV from Pillar C\n- **Thu (TikTok):** quick tip / listicle from Pillar A\n- **Fri (LinkedIn):** behind-the-scenes / build-in-public from Pillar B\n- **Fri (TikTok):** reactive/trend slot (left open)\n\nEach slot is a **standing instruction**, not a fixed post. Filling a slot with this week's actual\npost is the job of `batch-content-plan`. The skeleton is reusable every cycle.\n\n## Map pillars × formats × slots\n\nBuild the grid from what's already decided:\n\n- **Pillars** (from `content-pillars`) — what you talk about. Distribute slots across pillars so no\n  theme dominates and your signature pillar gets prime real estate.\n- **Formats** — how it's delivered (carousel, Reel/TikTok, thread, LinkedIn post, etc.), matched to\n  the platform and the pillar.\n- **Slots** — which day/platform. Spread formats so the feed has variety.\n\nThe grid guarantees pillar balance and format variety *by design*, instead of hoping for it.\n\n## Recurring series / franchises\n\nThe strongest calendars run **named recurring series** — a repeatable franchise in a fixed slot:\n\n- \"Teardown Tuesday\" (you critique something each week)\n- \"#FixItFriday\" (one common mistake + the fix)\n- \"Build Log\" (weekly build-in-public update)\n\nWhy they work: **anticipation** (audiences return for the next one), **less decision fatigue** (the\nformat's decided — just fill it), and **brand recognition** (the series becomes a thing you're known\nfor). Aim for one or two franchises; don't make every slot a series.\n\n## Planned vs reactive — leave room\n\nA calendar that's 100% pre-planned can't ride a trend, react to news, or jump on a moment — and\nthose are often the highest-reach posts. Reserve capacity for **reactive content**:\n\n- A rough split: **~70–80% planned/evergreen, ~20–30% reactive/timely.**\n- Keep one or two **open slots** per week explicitly for trend-jacking, news, or community moments\n  (hand the live trend research to `trend-jacking`).\n\nStructure gives you consistency; the reactive room gives you relevance. You need both.\n\n## Anchor known dates\n\nLayer time-bound anchors onto the recurring skeleton:\n\n- **Launches / announcements** — work backward from the date with teaser → launch → follow-up.\n- **Seasonal / industry events** — conferences, seasonal moments, recurring industry dates relevant\n  to *your* audience (not every generic holiday).\n- **Campaigns / series arcs** — a multi-week themed run.\n\nAnchors are the exception layer on top of the steady recurring rhythm — not a replacement for it.\n\n## The output\n\nA content calendar deliverable is: the **cadence** (per platform), the **recurring skeleton**\n(buckets by pillar × format × slot), the **series**, the **planned/reactive split**, and any\n**anchors** — plus a filled **sample week** to make it concrete. That's a reusable system, not a\ndisposable list.\n"
